A||N||7||MikeSchaefer||What does becoming an antique mean for Fieros?????||||4||
Z||000000||MikeSchaefer||04-09-2001||01:08 PM||||I was wandering how we will benefit when our Fieros start to become antiques.  Does it mean we can take off our cats & mufflers, paint our windows solid black, and run on leaded gas while dragging pianos down the road behind us?  There are a lot of wisetales about being an antique and I'd just like to know some facts about it.<BR>Thanks,<BR>Mike||||reg||4
Z||000001||rogergarrison||04-09-2001||01:24 PM||||in Ohio, 25 years old is all it needs to be an antique. as far as emissions/safety equipment-has to have same or better as originally came from factory.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||04-09-2001||02:25 PM||||In Illinois, The antique class is:<BR>Vehicle must be 25 years old.<BR>Antique plates can then be applied for.<BR>Once you get the plates you have rules to follow. First, the car can be insured for appraised value. You are exempt from emission testing. The vehicle can ONLY be driven to and from shows, repair shops and in Parades. You are limited to 500 miles per year and cannot use the vehicle for daily driving.<P>However, some insurance companies have a collectable/showcar status. The vehicle is insured for appraised value and you are exempt from emission testing. You cannot use the car for daily to and from work car but there is no mileage restriction or when you can drive it. For collectable status the car has to be 15 years or older and have an appraised value greater than $5000.00<BR>My car is insured as a colletable and is limited to 3400 miles by my insurance company. (If over 3400 miles the rate is higher) For showcar status you need to show you attended more than 2 carshows thru-out the year. Which for Fiero's is very easy to do. All you have to do is show proof that you attended it. (copy of registration form or pics of the car at the show)||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||04-09-2001||02:55 PM||||New York must be like Illinois..Antique 25 years old.   Collectible 15 years old, with those same limitations as to driving.  I had the collectible insurance put on my 87, which costs me $22/month.  A lot less than as a pleasure car which was ~$50/month and with $1000 deductible.||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||04-09-2001||03:45 PM||||Ive checked into some collector/replica car insurance. Its cheap enough but I dont like the restrictions, I wanna drive "MY' car when and where I want. One guy was really pressuring me to buy his, and I told him I only drive it for pleasure, but lots of miles. To me going on a date and driving it is pleasure so I told him to get lost. I dont want to own a 'garage queen'<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfixit58||04-09-2001||03:54 PM||||It means you're old!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Oh, sorry, your talking about the car!<P>Sorry, I couldn't resist.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000006||Hank is Here||04-09-2001||04:29 PM||||One thing that I hate about antique insurance is that you have to be 25 years old yourself to get a policy. My antiques are in my father name for insurance,  and he "gives" me permission each time I drive the vehicle.  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||rockcrawl||04-09-2001||09:34 PM||||Hank,<BR> I had the same problem with the insurance. I finished my Lambo when I was like 22 yrs old and I couldn't get insurance on it. I called every specialty car insurance company in the country, they all said I need to be 26 or have 10 years clean driving record. I ended up having a $30,000 car registered and insured as a '85 Fiero. Talk about being afraid to drive it. I drove it for a year and then sold it.<BR> My next Fiero will be registered as a Classic, no emisions testing, and only drive one day a week.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
